<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saxe-Merseburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1656–<a href="/years/1738">1738</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> was a duchy created in 1656–1657 for Christian, a younger son of Elector John George I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and centred on Merseburg on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>. It was one of three Albertine secondary lines, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a> and Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a>, whose dukes remained under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the electorate. When its line died out in 1738, the territory reverted to the Electorate of Saxony.</p></div>
